{"id": "e563b577-c126-42ec-b45f-693679c463eb", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "This EKS compliance check could pull a lot of data across clusterrolebindings, roles, and pods, which makes it a good candidate to delegate since the subagent's token-inefficient work is exactly this kind of scan. I'm checking though whether the subagent actually has kubectl access, since the listed tools seem to only include use_aws.

I think I do have kubectl access myself, so I can run it directly using custom-columns or jsonpath output to keep things compact rather than needing jmespath filtering. I'm mapping out the CIS EKS benchmark controls I need to check — things like cluster-admin role usage, wildcard permissions in RBAC, privileged container admission, host namespace sharing, CNI version, network policies per namespace, and default namespace usage — so I can plan parallel kubectl calls against each of these areas.

"}]}

{"id": "282164f3-2292-45d4-b1b2-ea2fb9316391", "role": "assistant", "block_type": "thinking", "content": [{"type": "text", "text": "I'm putting together the findings: 4.1.1/4.1.3 couldn't be evaluated, and 4.5.2 flags the default namespace containing legacy-web workloads (excluding built-in exempt resources), with secrets/SAs/netpols reads denied but not critical to the finding. For 4.2.1, I'm noting the privileged pod in team-a (priv-pod with a privileged pause container) plus checking kube-system for similar issues.

I also found hostNetwork usage (4.2.4) in team-a/hostnet-pod alongside expected system add-ons like aws-guardduty-agent, aws-node, and kube-proxy. I'm flagging that this pod listing is point-in-time and I never checked namespace labels for PSA/policy admission control, which is worth caveating. For 4.4.1, the kube-proxy version is outdated (v1.22.4-eksbuild.10 vs latest v1.23.3-eksbuild.2) and appears self-managed rather than installed as an EKS add-on, which is also worth calling out.

"}]}
